  ($id$int-m-89$id$, $sec$calculo-integral$sec$, $sub$inmediatas$sub$, $law$inmediatas$law$, $diff$medium$diff$, $prompt$Calcula mediante cambio de variable: $\int e^{7x}\,dx$$prompt$, $json$[{"latex":"e^{7x} + C","correct":false},{"latex":"\\frac{1}{49}e^{7x} + C","correct":false},{"latex":"7e^{7x} + C","correct":false},{"latex":"\\frac{1}{7}e^{7x} + C","correct":true}]$json$::jsonb, $exp$Con $u = 7x$, $du = 7\,dx$. La integral resulta $\frac{1}{7}e^{7x} + C$.$exp$),
  ($id$int-m-90$id$, $sec$calculo-integral$sec$, $sub$inmediatas$sub$, $law$inmediatas$law$, $diff$medium$diff$, $prompt$Calcula: $\int \frac{2x}{x^2 + 1}\,dx$$prompt$, $json$[{"latex":"2\\ln(x^2 + 1) + C","correct":false},{"latex":"\\frac{1}{x^2 + 1} + C","correct":false},{"latex":"\\frac{1}{2}\\ln(x^2 + 1) + C","correct":false},{"latex":"\\ln(x^2 + 1) + C","correct":true}]$json$::jsonb, $exp$Tomando $u = x^2 + 1$, su diferencial es $du = 2x\,dx$. La integral se reduce a $\int \frac{du}{u} = \ln|u| + C = \ln(x^2 + 1) + C$.$exp$),
  ($id$int-m-91$id$, $sec$calculo-integral$sec$, $sub$inmediatas$sub$, $law$inmediatas$law$, $diff$medium$diff$, $prompt$Calcula usando cambio de variable: $\int x(x^2 + 2)^{5}\,dx$$prompt$, $json$[{"latex":"\\frac{(x^2 + 2)^{5}}{12} + C","correct":false},{"latex":"\\frac{x^2(x^2 + 2)^{6}}{6} + C","correct":false},{"latex":"\\frac{(x^2 + 2)^{6}}{6} + C","correct":false},{"latex":"\\frac{(x^2 + 2)^{6}}{12} + C","correct":true}]$json$::jsonb, $exp$Con $u = x^2 + 2$, $du = 2x\,dx \implies x\,dx = \frac{du}{2}$. Entonces $\frac{1}{2}\int u^{5}\,du = \frac{u^{6}}{12} + C$.$exp$),
  ($id$int-m-92$id$, $sec$calculo-integral$sec$, $sub$inmediatas$sub$, $law$inmediatas$law$, $diff$medium$diff$, $prompt$Calcula mediante sustitución: $\int \cos(4x)\,dx$$prompt$, $json$[{"latex":"4\\sin(4x) + C","correct":false},{"latex":"\\sin(4x) + C","correct":false},{"latex":"-\\frac{1}{4}\\sin(4x) + C","correct":false},{"latex":"\\frac{1}{4}\\sin(4x) + C","correct":true}]$json$::jsonb, $exp$Con $u = 4x$, $du = 4\,dx \implies dx = \frac{du}{4}$, por tanto $\int \cos(4x)\,dx = \frac{1}{4}\sin(4x) + C$.$exp$),
  ($id$int-m-93$id$, $sec$calculo-integral$sec$, $sub$inmediatas$sub$, $law$inmediatas$law$, $diff$medium$diff$, $prompt$Calcula mediante cambio de variable: $\int e^{5x}\,dx$$prompt$, $json$[{"latex":"5e^{5x} + C","correct":false},{"latex":"e^{5x} + C","correct":false},{"latex":"\\frac{1}{25}e^{5x} + C","correct":false},{"latex":"\\frac{1}{5}e^{5x} + C","correct":true}]$json$::jsonb, $exp$Con $u = 5x$, $du = 5\,dx$. La integral resulta $\frac{1}{5}e^{5x} + C$.$exp$),
  ($id$int-m-94$id$, $sec$calculo-integral$sec$, $sub$inmediatas$sub$, $law$inmediatas$law$, $diff$medium$diff$, $prompt$Calcula: $\int \frac{2x}{x^2 + 5}\,dx$$prompt$, $json$[{"latex":"\\frac{1}{2}\\ln(x^2 + 5) + C","correct":false},{"latex":"2\\ln(x^2 + 5) + C","correct":false},{"latex":"\\frac{1}{x^2 + 5} + C","correct":false},{"latex":"\\ln(x^2 + 5) + C","correct":true}]$json$::jsonb, $exp$Tomando $u = x^2 + 5$, su diferencial es $du = 2x\,dx$. La integral se reduce a $\int \frac{du}{u} = \ln|u| + C = \ln(x^2 + 5) + C$.$exp$),
  ($id$int-m-95$id$, $sec$calculo-integral$sec$, $sub$inmediatas$sub$, $law$inmediatas$law$, $diff$medium$diff$, $prompt$Calcula usando cambio de variable: $\int x(x^2 + 1)^{5}\,dx$$prompt$, $json$[{"latex":"\\frac{(x^2 + 1)^{6}}{6} + C","correct":false},{"latex":"\\frac{(x^2 + 1)^{5}}{12} + C","correct":false},{"latex":"\\frac{x^2(x^2 + 1)^{6}}{6} + C","correct":false},{"latex":"\\frac{(x^2 + 1)^{6}}{12} + C","correct":true}]$json$::jsonb, $exp$Con $u = x^2 + 1$, $du = 2x\,dx \implies x\,dx = \frac{du}{2}$. Entonces $\frac{1}{2}\int u^{5}\,du = \frac{u^{6}}{12} + C$.$exp$),
  ($id$int-m-96$id$, $sec$calculo-integral$sec$, $sub$inmediatas$sub$, $law$inmediatas$law$, $diff$medium$diff$, $prompt$Calcula mediante sustitución: $\int \cos(2x)\,dx$$prompt$, $json$[{"latex":"2\\sin(2x) + C","correct":false},{"latex":"\\sin(2x) + C","correct":false},{"latex":"-\\frac{1}{2}\\sin(2x) + C","correct":false},{"latex":"\\frac{1}{2}\\sin(2x) + C","correct":true}]$json$::jsonb, $exp$Con $u = 2x$, $du = 2\,dx \implies dx = \frac{du}{2}$, por tanto $\int \cos(2x)\,dx = \frac{1}{2}\sin(2x) + C$.$exp$),
  ($id$int-m-97$id$, $sec$calculo-integral$sec$, $sub$inmediatas$sub$, $law$inmediatas$law$, $diff$medium$diff$, $prompt$Calcula mediante cambio de variable: $\int e^{3x}\,dx$$prompt$, $json$[{"latex":"e^{3x} + C","correct":false},{"latex":"\\frac{1}{9}e^{3x} + C","correct":false},{"latex":"3e^{3x} + C","correct":false},{"latex":"\\frac{1}{3}e^{3x} + C","correct":true}]$json$::jsonb, $exp$Con $u = 3x$, $du = 3\,dx$. La integral resulta $\frac{1}{3}e^{3x} + C$.$exp$),
  ($id$int-m-98$id$, $sec$calculo-integral$sec$, $sub$inmediatas$sub$, $law$inmediatas$law$, $diff$medium$diff$, $prompt$Calcula: $\int \frac{2x}{x^2 + 9}\,dx$$prompt$, $json$[{"latex":"2\\ln(x^2 + 9) + C","correct":false},{"latex":"\\frac{1}{x^2 + 9} + C","correct":false},{"latex":"\\frac{1}{2}\\ln(x^2 + 9) + C","correct":false},{"latex":"\\ln(x^2 + 9) + C","correct":true}]$json$::jsonb, $exp$Tomando $u = x^2 + 9$, su diferencial es $du = 2x\,dx$. La integral se reduce a $\int \frac{du}{u} = \ln|u| + C = \ln(x^2 + 9) + C$.$exp$),
  ($id$int-m-99$id$, $sec$calculo-integral$sec$, $sub$inmediatas$sub$, $law$inmediatas$law$, $diff$medium$diff$, $prompt$Calcula usando cambio de variable: $\int x(x^2 + 5)^{5}\,dx$$prompt$, $json$[{"latex":"\\frac{(x^2 + 5)^{6}}{6} + C","correct":false},{"latex":"\\frac{(x^2 + 5)^{5}}{12} + C","correct":false},{"latex":"\\frac{x^2(x^2 + 5)^{6}}{6} + C","correct":false},{"latex":"\\frac{(x^2 + 5)^{6}}{12} + C","correct":true}]$json$::jsonb, $exp$Con $u = x^2 + 5$, $du = 2x\,dx \implies x\,dx = \frac{du}{2}$. Entonces $\frac{1}{2}\int u^{5}\,du = \frac{u^{6}}{12} + C$.$exp$)
